\details{
Wraps the ESPN core-v2 endpoint
\code{sports.core.api.espn.com/v2/sports/football/leagues/college-football/seasons/{year}/types/{season_type}/athletes/{athlete_id}/statistics}.
ESPN nests a player's season stats under
\code{splits -> categories[] -> stats[]}; this wrapper flattens that tree to
long format -- each row is one stat, tagged with its \code{category} (e.g.
\code{passing}, \code{rushing}, \code{defensive}). The long shape is deliberate: ESPN
adds and retires stats and whole categories across positions and
seasons, and a long frame absorbs that drift without breaking column
expectations. Pivot wider with \code{\link[tidyr:pivot_wider]{tidyr::pivot_wider()}} keyed on
\code{stat_name} for a wide table. Each stat carries both a season-total
value (\code{value}) and a per-game value (\code{per_game_value}).

When \code{athlete_detail = TRUE} (the default) the requested athlete's ESPN
record is fetched once and the human-readable name columns
\code{athlete_display_name}, \code{athlete_first_name}, \code{athlete_last_name},
\code{athlete_jersey}, \code{athlete_position}, and \code{athlete_position_abbreviation}
are appended to every row. This is a single cheap fetch -- the wrapper
already takes one \code{athlete_id}. A fetch failure degrades the name columns
to \code{NA} rather than erroring the wrapper. Set \code{athlete_detail = FALSE} to
skip the fetch and reproduce the prior output exactly.
